  "salahTrainerNoteTakbirHanafi": "Raise the hands to the ears for the opening takbir, then fold them below the navel, right over left.",
  "salahTrainerNoteTakbirShafii": "Raise the hands to shoulder level, then fold them on the chest or just below it, right over left.",
  "salahTrainerNoteTakbirMaliki": "Raise the hands to shoulder level. In the obligatory prayer many Malikis then let the arms rest at the sides (sadl); folding them is also accepted.",
  "salahTrainerNoteTakbirHanbali": "Raise the hands to shoulder level, then fold them below the navel, right over left.",
  "salahTrainerNoteOpeningHanafi": "Recite Subhanaka quietly before al-Fatihah, in the first rakah only.",
  "salahTrainerNoteOpeningShafii": "The opening dua commonly taught is Wajjahtu wajhiya, recited quietly in the first rakah.",
  "salahTrainerNoteOpeningMaliki": "No opening dua is recited; after the takbir, go straight to al-Fatihah.",
  "salahTrainerNoteOpeningHanbali": "Recite Subhanaka quietly before al-Fatihah, in the first rakah only.",
  "salahTrainerNoteFatihahHanafi": "Say the bismillah quietly before reciting, and say Amin quietly at the end.",
  "salahTrainerNoteFatihahShafii": "The bismillah is part of al-Fatihah and is recited aloud in the loud prayers; say Amin aloud after it.",
  "salahTrainerNoteFatihahMaliki": "The bismillah is commonly left out in the obligatory prayer; say Amin quietly.",
  "salahTrainerNoteFatihahHanbali": "Say the bismillah quietly before reciting, and say Amin aloud in the loud prayers.",
  "salahTrainerNoteRukuHanafi": "Bow without raising the hands. Grip the knees with the fingers spread and keep the back level.",
  "salahTrainerNoteRukuShafii": "Raise the hands to the shoulders with the takbir as you bow, then grip the knees and keep the back level.",
  "salahTrainerNoteRukuMaliki": "Bow without raising the hands, grip the knees, and keep the back level.",
  "salahTrainerNoteRukuHanbali": "Raise the hands to the shoulders with the takbir as you bow, then grip the knees and keep the back level.",
  "salahTrainerNoteSittingHanafi": "Sit on the left foot with the right foot upright (iftirash) in every sitting.",
  "salahTrainerNoteSittingShafii": "Sit on the left foot with the right foot upright (iftirash) here and in the first tashahhud.",
  "salahTrainerNoteSittingMaliki": "Sit with the left hip on the ground and the feet out to the right (tawarruk) in every sitting.",
  "salahTrainerNoteSittingHanbali": "Sit on the left foot with the right foot upright (iftirash) here and in the first tashahhud.",
  "salahTrainerNoteTashahhudHanafi": "Raise the index finger at ’la ilaha’ and lower it at ’illa Allah’. The final sitting is also in iftirash.",
  "salahTrainerNoteTashahhudShafii": "Raise the index finger at ’illa Allah’ and keep it raised. In the final tashahhud sit in tawarruk, the left hip on the ground.",
  "salahTrainerNoteTashahhudMaliki": "Move the index finger gently from side to side through the tashahhud, seated in tawarruk.",
  "salahTrainerNoteTashahhudHanbali": "Point the index finger whenever Allah’s name is said. Sit in tawarruk only in the final tashahhud of a prayer with two.",
  "salahTrainerNoteTaslimHanafi": "Both salams are required (wajib); turn the head fully to each side.",
  "salahTrainerNoteTaslimShafii": "The first salam completes the prayer; the second is sunnah.",
  "salahTrainerNoteTaslimMaliki": "A single salam to the right completes the prayer; Malikis do not add a second.",
  "salahTrainerNoteTaslimHanbali": "Both salams are required; turn the head fully to each side.",
  "salahTrainerNoteFajrQunutShafii": "Qunut in Fajr is a confirmed sunnah: recite it after rising from ruku in the second rakah, with the hands raised.",
  "salahTrainerNoteFajrQunutMaliki": "Qunut in Fajr is recommended: recite it quietly before ruku in the second rakah.",
  "salahTrainerStepOpeningWajjahtuTranslation": "I have turned my face toward the One who created the heavens and the earth, inclining to truth, and I am not of those who associate others with Allah. My prayer, my rites, my living and my dying are for Allah, Lord of the worlds. He has no partner; so I have been commanded, and I am of the Muslims.",
